Description: Fix CVE-2018-7489 — block deserialization gadget classes at the polymorphic type-id resolution choke point
 jackson-databind 2.3.1 resolves attacker-controlled fully-qualified class
 names to Java Class instances during default-typing deserialization with no
 denylist at all. This allows unauthenticated remote code execution via known
 gadget chains (CVE-2017-7525) and, in particular, the c3p0
 com.mchange.v2.c3p0.*DataSource gadgets that make up CVE-2018-7489 (the
 incomplete-fix follow-up). Version 2.3.1 predates the upstream SubTypeValidator
 subsystem (added in 2.8.11 / 2.9), so this backport introduces an equivalent
 defensive denylist directly in ClassNameIdResolver._typeFromId(), the single
 choke point through which both Id.CLASS (ClassNameIdResolver) and
 Id.MINIMAL_CLASS (MinimalClassNameIdResolver, which delegates to
 super._typeFromId) flow. Dangerous class names / package prefixes are rejected
 with an IllegalArgumentException before any Class is resolved or specialized.
 .
 The denylist mirrors upstream SubTypeValidator.validateSubType: an exact-match
 set of well-known nasty classes, the org.springframework. Abstract*Advisor /
 Abstract*ApplicationContext handling, and the com.mchange.v2.c3p0.* + *DataSource
 rule that specifically addresses CVE-2018-7489. Set/HashSet/Collections are
 already imported via the existing java.util.* wildcard import.
Origin: ai-synthesized, CVE-2018-7489
Author: Root.io Novel Patcher <security@root.io>
Last-Update: 2026-08-20

